<pre class=metadata>
Title: Invoking Annotations: A Compile-Time Callback on Annotated Declarations
Shortname: Pnnnn
Level: 0
Status: D
Group: WG21
Date: 2026-09-23
Audience: SG7, EWG, LEWG
Editor: Ian Pike, ianpike98cs@gmail.com
Editor Term: Reply-to, Reply-to
Abstract: C++26 annotations expose stored values through reflection but leave libraries to arrange declaration-dependent checks and computations separately.
Abstract:
Abstract: This paper proposes mandatory compile-time annotation callbacks that retain supported results for later retrieval. We request direction on invocation and retained results independently of direct-injection permission, while preserving indirect injection through a specialization's `consteval` block. An annotation opts in through a callee's declared `std::meta::annotation_context` parameter without body inspection, preserving existing C++26 behavior. Each occurrence is invoked once for each declaration or base-specifier it annotates, changing neither its input nor its target.
Abstract:
Abstract: Retention adds result associations, publication rules, and module handling. Void callbacks produce no result. Supported by-value results become immutable, reflectable data for the occurrence and target after invocation, capture, and cleanup succeed. Later queries retrieve those results without repeating the computation.
Abstract:
Abstract: Scheduling for declarations that define both a type and an object, annotated lambdas, and dependent specializations remains unresolved, as does correspondence across translation units and imports.
Boilerplate: omit conformance, omit feedback-header
Markup Shorthands: markdown yes
